            <ArticleTitle>Lack of very strong association between pre-treatment fibrinogen and PAI-1 with long-term mortality after coronary bypass surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>82-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To explore the association between the coagulation protein fibrinogen and the fibrinolytic biomarker plasminogen activator inhibitor-1 (PAI-1) and the long-term mortality after coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">In 729 patients undergoing CABG at Sahlgrenska University Hospital, a blood sample for fibrinogen and PAI-1 was collected prior to the procedure. Patients were followed for 10 years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Among patients with high levels of fibrinogen (&gt;3.6 g/l; median), the 10-year mortality was 32.3 vs. 20.7% among patients with fibrinogen levels below the median (p = 0.0005). However, patients with higher levels of fibrinogen were older and had an adverse risk factor pattern. When adjusting for these differences, pre-operative fibrinogen levels did not clearly appear as an independent predictor of long-term mortality. The 10-year mortality was similar in patients with high (25.3%) and low (26.5%) levels of PAI-1.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Our results do not suggest that fibrinogen and PAI-1, when evaluated prior to the operative procedure, are strongly associated with increased mortality in the long-term after CABG, when other co-morbidity factors are simultaneously considered.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>2007 S. Karger AG, Basel</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
